{"records":[{"id":"bn23yc7cwv","title":"NEK2 is a potential pan-cancer biomarker and immunotherapy target","description":"NEK2 may be a potential pan-cancer biomarker and immunotherapeutic target for improving the efficacy of tumor therapy. This study investigated the expression level, prognostic value, gene mutations, MSI, TMB, immune checkpoints and related signalling pathways of NEK2 in pan-cancer using a comprehensive bioinformatics analysis. The results indicated that NEK2 overexpression has a certain diagnostic value in a variety of cancer types; NEK2 may be a potential prognostic and immune-related biomarker for cancer patients. This study elucidated the role of NEK2 in tumorigenesis from multiple perspectives, and provided some bases for further research on the specific mechanisms of NEK2 in cancer progression and treatment. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-05-15T06:57:34.18Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bn23yc7cwv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bn23yc7cwv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lanyue","last_name":"zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yang","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wenxin","last_name":"Liao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tingting","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Juexiao","last_name":"Deng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fujin","last_name":"Shen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Medicine","Bioinformatics","Cervical Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9h2jwrnjkw","title":"Raw Data","description":"Raw Data____VCAN as a Potential Prognostic Biomarker and Mediator of Tumor Microenvironment Remodeling in Gastric Adenocarcinoma","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-04-30T07:27:29.262Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9h2jwrnjkw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9h2jwrnjkw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xiangchen","last_name":"Qiu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Medical Biotechnology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y7x58f8ww8","title":"Rat_Corneal_Endothelial_Cells_EndMT_Single-Cell_RNA_Seq","description":"single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq)  in a rat corneal endothelial injury and endothelial-to-mesenchymal transition (EndMT) model, linked Spp1 upregulation to EndMT. We try to establish a connection between SPP1 upregulation and guttae formation, to find a potential biomarker and therapeutic target for FECD. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-03-24T10:16:32.376Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y7x58f8ww8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y7x58f8ww8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fuji","last_name":"Deng","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"82271055"}],"categories":["Cornea","Rat","Corneal Endothelium"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8fjcgshgxc","title":"Data of microRNAs and Target Gene in Preconditioned compared to Naive Extracellular Vesicles Derived from Umbilical Cord Mesenchymal Stem Cells","description":"This dataset contains information on the microRNAs and their target genes found in extracellular vesicles (EVs) derived from umbilical cord mesenchymal stem cells (UC-MSCs). The data compares microRNA profiles between preconditioned and naive (non-preconditioned) EVs. Data Applications:\n- Therapeutic Potential: Understanding how preconditioning alters microRNA content in EVs can help in designing better therapeutic strategies using UC-MSC-derived EVs.\n- Mechanistic Insights: Insights into how preconditioned EVs affect target gene expression can elucidate mechanisms of action in various physiological and pathological processes.\n- Biomarker Discovery: Identification of specific microRNAs and target genes can serve as biomarkers for monitoring the efficacy of preconditioning treatments.\nThis dataset is crucial for researchers focusing on the therapeutic use of EVs, particularly in enhancing the regenerative potential of UC-MSCs through preconditioning strategies.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-08-05T18:57:18.676Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8fjcgshgxc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8fjcgshgxc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ratna","last_name":"Puspita","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Clinical Biochemistry","Mesenchymal Stem Cell","Transcriptomics","Nanomedicine","Extracellular Vesicle","Biomedical Research","Exosome"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"682mmrvgv2","title":"A study of Therapeutic and Prognostic Potential of GPCRs in Prostate Cancer From Multi-Omics Landscape, Shiqi Li et al.","description":"Original data for Figures in \"Therapeutic and Prognostic Potential of GPCRs in Prostate Cancer From Multi-Omics Landscape\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-07-04T19:22:44.599Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/682mmrvgv2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/682mmrvgv2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Shiqi","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Bioinformatics","Prostate Cancer","G Protein-Coupled Receptor","Omics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8wk52dmb2r","title":"Acyltransferase ZDHHC22 promotes N-Myc transcriptional activation to drive neuroblastoma progression and chemoresistance","description":"MYCN-amplified neuroblastoma is one of the most lethal pediatric malignancies, where aberrant N-Myc-driven transcription promotes tumor progression. As direct targeting of N-Myc has proven challenging, current approaches prioritize understanding and modulating the mechanisms that regulate its activity, which remain poorly understood. Here, we demonstrate a crucial role of S-acylation in the regulation of N-Myc transcriptional activity and identify the acyltransferase ZDHHC22 as a key regulator of this process. Mechanistically, ZDHHC22 catalyzes the S-acylation of N-Myc, which enhances its transcriptional activity by facilitating the recruitment of transcriptional coactivators such as TIP60 and GCN5. Furthermore, N-Myc transcriptionally upregulates ZDHHC22, establishing a positive feedback loop that contributes to chemoresistance in high-risk neuroblastoma. Targeting ZDHHC22 suppresses neuroblastoma cell growth in vitro and in vivo, particularly in refractory patient-derived models. Collectively, our findings not only uncover a new biological function of ZDHHC22 in regulating N-Myc transcriptional activation but also indicate that ZDHHC22 is a promising therapeutic target for N-Myc-driven high-risk neuroblastoma, especially MYCN-amplified patients.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-04-20T17:53:01.875Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8wk52dmb2r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8wk52dmb2r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Meidan","last_name":"Ying","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Neuroblastoma","Transcription Factor"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ydf4zt5hzb","title":"MICALL2, MLXIPL, and MXD3 Link Serum Uric Acid to Immune Evasion and Drug Sensitivity in Prostate Cancer","description":"MICALL2, MLXIPL, and MXD3 may promote PCa progression through metabolic–immune crosstalk and serve as potential prognostic biomarkers and predictors of therapeutic response, offering novel insights for precision treatment strategies.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-09-03T19:03:37.354Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ydf4zt5hzb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ydf4zt5hzb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jinhai","last_name":"Wu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Oncology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"g6zhdsc2tc","title":"CTHRC1 is a new therapeutic target and serum diagnostic biomarker for aortic dissection","description":"Aortic dissection (AD) is a cardiovascular disease with rapid onset and an alarmingly high short-term mortality. Currently, there are no specific and rapid detection biomarkers based on peripheral blood or effective medications. In this study, we analyzed AD samples from mouse models and human patients, revealing a significant elevation of vascular fibroblast-derived exocrine protein CTHRC1 in the blood. Furthermore, single-cell analysis indicated that CTHRC1 regulates the phenotypic transformation of vascular smooth muscle cells (VSMCs) during arterial remodeling. By binding to ADAM9 receptor on the VSMC membrane, CTHRC1 activates the ERK1/2 signaling pathway, promoting the transformation of VSMCs from a contractile phenotype to a synthetic phenotype. Genetic ablation of CTHRC1 or blockade using anti-CTHRC1 monoclonal antibodies effectively prevented AD development in Ang-II/BAPN-induced mouse models. These findings unveil the critical role of CTHRC1 in regulating VSMC phenotypic switch and maintaining aortic structural integrity through the ERK1/2 pathway. Thus, CTHRC1 represents a novel serum diagnostic biomarker and a promising therapeutic target for AD.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-02T12:08:11.264Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/g6zhdsc2tc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/g6zhdsc2tc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"jiaxin","last_name":"chen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Fibroblast","Aortic Dissection","Smooth Muscle Cell"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"cps9nsmjbg","title":"AMIGO2","description":"AMIGO2 is a novel transmembrane protein involved in neuronal growth and development, playing a significant role in various cancers. This study systematically analyzed the expression of AMIGO2 in pan-cancer and its correlations with clinical prognosis, immune checkpoints, tumor purity scores, and immune infiltration by integrating databases such as TCGA, GTEx, and cBioPortal. The results showed that AMIGO2 is upregulated in most tumor tissues, and its high expression is associated with poor prognosis in certain cancers. AMIGO2 was significantly correlated with immune regulatory genes, immune checkpoints, and tumor purity scores, suggesting its potential role in the tumor immune microenvironment. Additionally, low AMIGO2 expression was linked to better responses to specific immunotherapies, such as Ipilimumab, Nivolumab, and anti-CTLA4 treatments. This study highlights the potential of AMIGO2 as a prognostic biomarker and immunotherapy target, providing new insights for precision cancer treatment.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-03-19T15:32:21.803Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cps9nsmjbg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cps9nsmjbg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"yujie","last_name":"Di","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biological Database","Database"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vxyy62m2c7","title":"Profiling of ERBB receptors and downstream pathways reveals selectivity and hidden properties of ERBB4 antagonists","description":"ERBB receptor tyrosine kinases are involved in development and diseases like cancer, cardiovascular, neurodevelopmental, and mental disorders. Although existing drugs target ERBB receptors, the next generation of drugs requires enhanced selectivity and understanding of physiological pathway responses to improve efficiency and reduce side effects. To address this, we developed a multi-level barcoded reporter profiling assay, termed ‘ERBBprofiler’, in living cells to monitor the activity of all ERBB targets and key physiological pathways simultaneously. This assay helps differentiate on-target therapeutic effects from off-target and off-pathway side effects of ERBB antagonists. To challenge the assay, eight established ERBB antagonists were profiled. Known effects were confirmed, and previously uncharacterized properties were discovered, such as pyrotinib's preference for ERBB4 over EGFR. Additionally, two lead compounds selectively targeting ERBB4 were profiled, showing promise for clinical trials. Taken together, this multiparametric profiling approach can guide early-stage drug development and lead to improved future therapeutic interventions.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-01-11T07:35:52.919Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vxyy62m2c7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vxyy62m2c7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Luksa","last_name":"Popovic","orcid":"0000-0002-6358-7801"},{"first_name":"Jan","last_name":"Wintgens","orcid":"0000-0002-1255-6837"},{"first_name":"Yuxin","last_name":"Wu","orcid":"0000-0002-1586-6160"},{"first_name":"Ben","last_name":"Brankatschk","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sascha","last_name":"Menninger","orcid":"0000-0002-3665-3973"},{"first_name":"Carsten","last_name":"Degenhart","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Niels","last_name":"Jensen","orcid":"0000-0001-8171-3333"},{"first_name":"Sven","last_name":"Wichert","orcid":"0000-0003-4052-2711"},{"first_name":"Bert","last_name":"Klebl","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Moritz","last_name":"Rossner","orcid":"0000-0002-0667-3420"},{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Wehr","orcid":"0000-0003-1770-8323"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Erlotinib","Gefitinib","Lapatinib","Poziotinib","Osimertinib","Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor","Serotonin Receptor","Receptor Tyrosine Kinase","Multiplexing"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":137775,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":138,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":137775,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":136997,"restricted":71,"embargoed":707},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2299},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":688},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":471},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":439},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":433},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":418},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":398},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":335},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":279},{"id":"https://ror.org/05r0vyz12","name":"Ministerio de Ciencia, Innovación y Universidades","count":231}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":135123},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1911},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":185},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":135},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":78},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":58},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":40},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"Univers